prudyntctl writes a body that starts with '{' to the socket as is, so the
RUNTIME verb from 0006 only reaches the server for bodies that do not.
imp-control and microphone always pass a JSON object, so their requests are
read as plain JSON and their paths are still staged: the next persist (a
privacy change with save_state, for instance) writes the LITE throttle and
any other runtime value into the user layer.

Send the verb whenever the runtime transport is requested. A bare body on
the plain transport keeps meaning plain JSON.

diff --git a/src/prudyntctl.cpp b/src/prudyntctl.cpp
index 88589ff..eaeb7f7 100644
--- a/src/prudyntctl.cpp
+++ b/src/prudyntctl.cpp
@@ -79,8 +79,10 @@ static int cmd_json(int argc, char **argv, bool runtime) {
     return 2;
   }
 
-  // Allow bare JSON body or prefixed with the transport verb
-  if (!payload.empty() && payload[0] == '{') {
+  // Allow bare JSON body or prefixed with the transport verb. A bare body is
+  // read as plain JSON by the server, so the runtime transport always sends
+  // its verb: imp-control and microphone pass bodies that start with '{'.
+  if (!runtime && !payload.empty() && payload[0] == '{') {
     // write JSON directly
     (void)write(fd, payload.c_str(), payload.size());
   } else {
